From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:36:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cdp: keep console Error descriptions inert without losing
 handles

V8's default Error mirror reads the built-in stack getter even when remote
object previews are disabled. This invokes Error.prepareStackTrace while
serializing Runtime.consoleAPICalled in both page and worker scopes.

Use the existing inspector client subtype/description callbacks only while
wrapping the specific native Error console argument. Match the inspector
client and original value and restore the thread-local scope afterward.
Keep V8's original object handle, Error subtype and class name, then supply
an inert description without touching .stack. Ordinary explicit Runtime
inspection and JavaScript stack reads retain their existing behavior.
Group console argument handles under console so normal object/group
release and session teardown retain native inspector ownership semantics.

Preserve safe own string Error names in inert formatting and ignore
non-string messages, avoiding coercion and recursive/cyclic Error message
traversal. Keep console methods, events and primitive handling unchanged.
No JavaScript injection, V8 dependency changes or new upstream tests.

Validation: Debug and ReleaseFast builds; all 1658 upstream tests passed.
Temporary probes cover nine page console methods, six worker methods,
Error subclasses and AggregateError, original-object identity, default and
custom stack formatting, throwing hooks, frozen/accessor errors, cyclic
messages, custom names, native console shape, explicit inspection, handle
release and Runtime toggling. One reviewer pass found cyclic-message and
custom-name issues; both were fixed and validated without a second pass.
The unchanged passive benchmark improves from 123/130 to 125/130 (96.15%),
fixing console-prepare-stack-trace and worker-console-prepare-stack-trace
with no regressions and no collection/network-evidence errors.
